--TEST--
Serialization follows the door: a tree the WHATWG parser recovered into is read like any other and refused where XML has no spelling for it — `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`, `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Html::parse` never fails, so a tree can hold what XML refuses. The two
// doors share the node family and not the writing rules, which is what this
// case pins: the recovered comment is readable through the same members every
// other node answers, and only writing it back out through the XML door stops.
Core\Xml\Node $comment = Core\Html::parse("<p>a<!--c--d--></p>");
array<Core\Xml\Node> $html = $comment->children();
array<Core\Xml\Node> $body = $html[0]->children();
array<Core\Xml\Node> $paragraph = $body[1]->children();
array<Core\Xml\Node> $inside = $paragraph[0]->children();
echo "read:    \"", $inside[1]->text(), "\"\n";

try {
    echo $comment->source(), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "refused: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A name is the other half: the HTML tokenizer keeps a quoted attribute name
// as the name it was written as, and no XML document can carry it.
Core\Xml\Node $attribute = Core\Html::parse("<p \"q\"=v>x</p>");
try {
    echo $attribute->source(), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $second) {
    echo "refused: ", $second->message, "\n";
}

// The two refusals meet: a tree the XML door parsed is always writable,
// because the parse refused the same construct on the way in.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<p><!--c--d--></p>");
} catch (ParseError $stopped) {
    echo "parse:   ", $stopped->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
read:    "c--d"
refused: Core\Xml\Node::source(): a comment has no escape grammar, so it cannot hold `--` or end with `-`.
refused: Core\Xml\Node::source(): `"q"` is not a name a document can write.
parse:   Core\Xml::parse(): a comment holds `--`, which XML does not allow inside one.
